<h2 id="news-hook">PocketBook's First Phone-Sized Reader Lands at IFA Berlin</h2>

<p>PocketBook has officially unveiled the <strong>PocketBook Q</strong> &mdash; the Swiss-rooted e-reader maker's first phone-sized reader, designed to slip into the same pocket as your phone and pull double-duty as an EPUB-first distraction-free device. The company <a href="https://pocketbook.de/en/news/new-at-ifa-2026-pocketbook-unveils-the-smartphone-sized-pocketbook-q-e-reader" rel="noopener">announced the Q on August 25, 2026</a>, ahead of its public debut at <strong>IFA Berlin 2026</strong> (Hall 5.2A, Booth 143, September 4&ndash;8).</p>

<p>PocketBook is pitching the Q at US$235 / &euro;199 / &pound;175, with shipping scheduled for <strong>Q4 2026</strong> (late fall). For EPUB readers who already carry a Calibre-managed library and want a pocket companion that <em>isn't</em> a smartphone, this is the most deliberate open-ecosystem entry into the phone-sized reader category yet &mdash; a category that the Boox Palma has owned on Android since 2023.</p>

<p>What makes the Q stand apart from the existing PocketBook lineup &mdash; particularly the 7-inch <a href="/blog/pocketbook-era-lite-open-ebook-reader-2026/">Era Lite we already covered</a> &mdash; is its scale and its stance. The Q is the smallest PocketBook to date at 5.84 inches and 130 grams, and the first to ship with a deliberately <strong>Linux-based, non-Android</strong> operating system. PocketBook is leaning into "leave your phone behind" rather than "make your phone into a reader."</p>

