<p>The single biggest difference for actual reading habits is the <strong>page-turn buttons</strong>. The Era Lite requires a thumb on the touchscreen to advance pages, which is fine if you read one-handed and don't mind covering half the screen with your thumb. The Q, with physical buttons on the side rim, lets you read in the rain, on the subway with gloves, or in bed lying on your side without needing a precise touch target. For commuters and one-handed readers, that alone justifies the $35 premium.</p>

<h2 id="q-vs-palma">PocketBook Q vs Boox Palma: The Open Question</h2>

<p>The PocketBook Q's most direct competitor is the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma</a>, which has owned the phone-sized reader conversation for the last two years. The two devices take opposite philosophical approaches to the same form factor:</p>

