<p>If you read EPUB files you have personally organized in <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">Calibre</a>, both devices handle them well: the Q via its native Linux format support, the Palma via any EPUB reader app from Google Play. If you also want the Kindle, Kobo, or Audible apps installed on the same device, the Palma is the only option &mdash; the Q simply does not have an app store. If, on the other hand, you have noticed that having Twitter and email on the same device you read on is making it harder to actually read, the Q's lack of an app store becomes a feature rather than a limitation.</p>

<h2 id="epub-workflow">Reading Your Existing EPUB Library on the PocketBook Q</h2>

<p>For anyone with an existing EPUB-first workflow, the Q slots into it cleanly. The three most common paths all work out of the box:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Sideload via USB-C.</strong> Connect the Q to a computer, drop EPUB files into the device's storage, and they appear in your library. Because PocketBook supports 25 formats natively, no conversion step is needed for EPUB, PDF, MOBI, FB2, DJVU, TXT, RTF, or HTML.</li>
  <li><strong>Send from Calibre.</strong> Use Calibre's "Connect to folder" or "Send to device" function to push books directly into the Q's library over USB. This is the workflow most Calibre users will already have running for other devices.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync from PocketBook Cloud.</strong> Sign into PocketBook Cloud on the Q, then upload EPUBs from the PocketBook Reader mobile app or the PocketBook desktop client, and they appear across all your PocketBook devices.</li>
</ol>

<p>The PocketBook Reader mobile app &mdash; available for iOS and Android &mdash; also handles cross-device bookmark and reading-position sync, so you can pick up an EPUB on your phone while commuting and continue on the Q when you sit down at home. For library users, the preinstalled Libby integration lets you borrow from your local library and read borrowed EPUBs without sideloading anything.</p>

<p>If you are coming from a Kindle, our <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">guide to migrating a Kindle library to EPUB</a> covers how to extract your existing books into open formats first, and our <a href="/blog/send-epub-to-kindle/">Send EPUB to Kindle</a> guide covers the reverse path for the books you still want to read on a Kindle. The Q is an EPUB-native device, so the migration path is more natural than the conversion path.</p>

<h2 id="caveats">What the Q Doesn't Have</h2>

<p>No device is perfect, and the Q has a few honest tradeoffs worth flagging. First, there is no microSD card slot mentioned in any source &mdash; 32 GB of internal storage is what you get, and that's it. For most readers, 32 GB holds thousands of EPUBs, but if you keep a large audiobook collection locally, it will fill up faster than you'd like. Second, there is no 3.5 mm headphone jack and no built-in speaker mentioned in the spec sheet &mdash; audio is Bluetooth 5.0 only or USB-C wired. Third, IPX4 is <em>splash and rain</em> resistant, not submersible: the Q can survive a downpour, but it should not go in the bathtub the way an IPX8-rated reader like the Era Lite can. Finally, the 1,550 mAh battery is on the smaller side for an E Ink device, though PocketBook's history of conservative battery tuning means the real-world number is likely closer to three to four weeks of moderate reading than the headline capacity suggests.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>The PocketBook Q is a focused, deliberately limited device, and that is its appeal. At $235 with a 5.84-inch Carta 1300 screen, physical page-turn buttons, Linux + preinstalled Libby, IPX4 splash resistance, and PocketBook's trademark 25-format native support, it is the first genuinely anti-smartphone phone-sized reader on the market. It does not try to be a tiny Android tablet, and it does not try to replace your Kindle. It reads EPUBs, it reads library books, it syncs your bookmarks, and it gets out of the way. For EPUB readers who already manage a Calibre library and want a pocket companion without distractions, the Q is the most interesting open-ecosystem reader of 2026. It ships Q4 2026.</p>

